  "complexity": 6,
  "title": "S3c memory feeds skills and strategy: recurring procedural memory becomes learned-skill candidates, promoted skills are referenced from memory, workflow strategy outcomes live as structured WARM records with a Planner query (P30, P31)",
  "spec": "orchestrator/skill_learning.py: add candidates_from_memory(root) using memory_store.search over kinds gotcha and decision: group records by shared components (at least 3 records over at least 2 distinct dates) whose bodies contain procedural language (imperative verbs such as run, check, set, use, never, always, before, after) and produce learned-skill proposals through the existing proposal path (same shape and quarantine/testing lifecycle as today's learned skills; provenance memory with the record ids). When a proposal's skill is promoted to active, memory_store.add records a tag skill:<skill_id> on each source record and memory_hot renders such records as one line pointing at the skill instead of the full body. orchestrator/strategy.py: at merge time (new hook: daemon.py joins the scope; in the merge path that marks a task merged, call strategy.record_outcome(task, state_dir=STATE) (daemon.py already binds the name root to failures.root; never shadow it) which computes the token, usd, duration and fix-round figures the way strategy.observations() does today and writes the record) write one memory_store record kind strategy per merged execute task: title '<task_class> <band> <strategy name>', tags task_class:<x>, band:<b>, strategy:<name>, outcome first_pass|fix_rounds:<n>, body with tokens, duration, executor, fix rounds; source_task and source_goal set. CLI `orchestrator memory strategies [--task-class x] [--band b] [--json]` aggregates strategy records: per strategy name the count, first-pass rate, median tokens and duration, so the Planner asks what worked for this kind of task without reading retrospectives. Docs: docs/hermes-hardening/17-memory-skills-strategy.md.\nSpec review T-1182 folded in: (1) The merge-time hook is new (daemon.py never called strategy.py): implement strategy.record_outcome(task, root) reusing the computation of strategy.observations(), called from the daemon's merge path (scope adds orchestrator/daemon.py and tests/test_daemon.py). (2) Tagging source records with skill:<skill_id> lives inside skills_registry.transition() when the new state is active (scope adds orchestrator/skills_registry.py), so both `skills promote` and the generic `skills transition` paths tag. (3) Proposals from memory get roles = the union of the role fields of the source records' source tasks (looked up on the bus when present) with 'execute' as the default, never an empty list; a test asserts a proposal is selectable for execute. (4) Record shape for strategy records: Record.outcome holds first_pass or fix_rounds:<n>; tags carry only task_class:<x>, band:<b>, strategy:<name>; `memory strategies` aggregates on those fields.\nSpec review T-1192 folded in: (1) root conventions are explicit: memory_store and memory_hot take the REPO root (Path(root)/'.orchestrator'/...), strategy, skill_learning and skills_registry take the .orchestrator STATE directory; when one calls the other, convert (state_dir.parent for the repo root) and say so in a comment; the tests follow each module's harness convention. (2) The merge hook resolves the lineage root first (attribution.lineage / strategy._root_id) so a fix-round merge records the outcome for the root task; one record per merged lineage root. (3) Strategy record id is deterministic and unique per task: id = 'strategy-<task_id>' passed explicitly to memory_store.add, never derived from kind/date/title. (4) Roles for memory-sourced proposals: read the source tasks' JSON from state_dir/tasks/<id>.json the way skill_learning._tasks does, never bus.get; missing files count as no role; default ['execute']. (5) Confidence for memory-sourced patterns: min(1.0, records/5) multiplied by min(1.0, distinct_dates/3), evidence_strength = record count; the existing propose() threshold (0.6) applies unchanged, so a group needs at least 3 records over at least 2 dates and typically 3 dates to pass; document the formula in 17-memory-skills-strategy.md.\nRepository rules: unittest (not pytest), tests/_harness.py single ORCH_ROOT fixture; every acceptance test id tests/test_<module>.py::test_name is a method def test_name(self) in a unittest.TestCase. No new third-party dependencies. Never write secret values to any file or log; test fixtures use synthetic names and values that are not credential-shaped. Do not quote template placeholder tokens (double braces) anywhere in code comments or docs. Shadow-first: a new adaptive path never changes what a worker sees until its mode is active; shadow only writes decision_log or runs rows. Task worktrees carry no .orchestrator runs, tasks or decision_log state: tests use synthetic fixtures; for any measured numbers in docs run read-only against /Users/k3ntaw/code/orchestrator/.orchestrator via an explicit root argument. Run .claude/hooks/tests-green.sh . until green; the base suite must stay green. Do not ask questions: decide, and note decisions in the result summary. Contracts of the earlier modules: .orchestrator/plan.md (section GOAL T-0991) and docs/hermes-hardening/*.md on this branch; read the merged modules before building on them.",
